## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical error handling hygiene and exposure risk in APIs.

## Key Concepts
- Centralized error handling
- Error classification (operational vs unknown)
- Sanitized client messages while preserving logs
- Consistent error shape for clients
- Testing for leakage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Express error middleware
const errorHandler = (err, req, res, next) => {
  if (!err.isOperational) {
    console.error(err);
    return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
  }
  res.status(err.statusCode || 400).json({ error: err.message || 'Bad request' });
};
app.use(errorHandler);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for async/await routes with a library like express-async-errors?
- What logging strategy would you use to preserve full details without exposing them to clients?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical, beginner-friendly security hygiene around logging, a common real-world risk.

## Key Concepts
- Redaction in middleware
- Structured logging with masking
- Access controls on log sinks
- CI gates for secrets leakage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example Express middleware to redact sensitive fields in req.body and req.query
function redactLogs(req, res, next) {
  const sensitive = ['email','password','token','authorization','ssn','user_id'];
  const redact = (obj) => {
    if (!obj) return obj;
    if (typeof obj !== 'object') return obj;
    for (const k of Object.keys(obj)) {
      if (sensitive.includes(k)) obj[k] = 'REDACTED';
      else if (typeof obj[k] === 'object') redact(obj[k]);
    }
    return obj;
  };
  req.redacted = redact(Object.assign({}, req.body, req.query));
  next();
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test log redaction across nested payloads?
- What changes if you switch to a distributed log system across services?

